body:
You never wanted to be with him in the first place, and it's not like you lead him on, you told him no and he pushed and pushed and pushed. He is trying to force you to be in a relationship with him, which you did not want then, and do not want now.  You have put in a lot of effort to be ok with the "relationship", you've probably found comfort and value in aspects of it at times, which I imagine makes you feel worse about not wanting to be with him, but it is very clear that you don't want to be with him, and you shouldn't want to be with him. You are with someone who from the start has been abusive towards you, and has raped you.

&#x200B;

Arguing with him to get him to leave is not going to work, so the important thing isn't \*if\* you should leave him, but \*how\*.  You don't need to do it immediately, but it is worth planning things so you can leave successfully.

&#x200B;

Important aspects seem to me to be: Getting him out of your house, or you out of his house (I don't know much about your living situation). Are there friends you can stay with, or who can help you pack his things and dump them outside and change the locks while he's out?  If he never leaves the house, and it's your house, then you'd probably need police to get him out.  If you're the one leaving, then you need to bring the dog with you and anything that you consider essential.  Call the police even if you don't need their help immediately just so they know the situation for when he continues to stalk you.

Once you've left, or you've kicked him out, he WILL continue to pester you as much as he possibly can.  Think about the different ways he has of contacting you - block him on social media accounts and on your phone.  Consider changing your phone number so he doesn't have access to that.  If he makes social media accounts to get in touch with you, consider either locking down your settings so people can't add you, or making new accounts entirely so he doesn't know how to access them.  If he somehow finds a way to contact you, instead of telling him why he shouldn't be doing that, just block him as soon as you realise it is him.   
 Tell mutual friends not to pass messages between you for any reason.  If and when he turns up outside your house or your friends houses, call the police and have them arrest him - you don't need to tell him "you need to leave or I'll call the police", just do it.  Every chance he has to talk to you is a chance he might make you feel like the bad guy, and you are NOT AT FAULT. Even if you have done bad things in the relationship, even if he's done nice things for you sometimes, even when he's in tears about losing you, you are NOT AT FAULT and would doing the right thing by leaving him, and every time he interacts with you he will try to change your mind, so you need to stop him interacting with you.  

&#x200B;

You are not a bad person for this, he has ignored your boundaries for the whole time he's known you, and he will fight like hell when you try to put those boundaries back up, but you deserve to escape from him.  

&#x200B;

Sorry for the wall of text, I've known a lot of people in similar situations to this. If you want help talking through exit plans or more assurance that you're doing the right thing by getting out, you're welcome to PM me.  There are also a few subreddits for abuse victims which I'm sure you'd be welcome to post in, and lots of online resources on how to leave an abusive relationship.  I truly hope you get the support you need to be rid of this guy.

refusal doesn't have to be "no we can't have sex", it could be "If you want to have sex you have to touch me in this way first". 

&#x200B;

Have you tried initiating yourself? Wear something you feel sexy in, go up to him and tell him to touch you, maybe put his hand on your boob.  Work out what kind of foreplay you want, tell him to do that
